	@FunctionalInterface
	public interface Predicate<T> {
	    boolean test(T t);
	}
	T → type of input parameter
	test(T t) → abstract method that evaluates the condition and returns a boolean
	
	In short :- 
		Predicate = A condition (T → boolean) that you can chain with
		and(), or(), negate(), and use widely in functional programming with Java.


-> A Predicate is a functional interface in Java that represents a boolean condition.
-> Think of it as a test/check: "Does this object satisfy the condition?"
-> It takes one input and always returns true or false.
-> You often use it in filtering, validations, and conditional logic with streams or collections.
	
	
-> When you need a boolean condition (true/false logic).
	->Common in:	
	->Stream filtering (filter())	
	->Validations	
	->Conditional execution

